<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muzaka family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Muzaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Muzaka were an Albanian noble family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myzeqe">Myzeqe plain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a>. In the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-ii-muzaka">Andrea II Muzaka</a> received the Byzantine title of despot, and the family ruled Berat until the Ottoman conquest of 1417. Its members later joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a>. In 1510 Gjon Muzaka, then in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, wrote a short history of the family, an important source for medieval Albania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muzaka-family.jpg" alt="Muzaka family" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Illustration by Kj1595 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stema_e_Muzakajve.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
